@*
    Everything a record states except its status: the labelled rows under the
    headline.

    A component of its own because the two halves of a record are not always drawn
    in the same place. In a document they are — the status closes the heading and
    the fields hang under it, which is what MetadataView composes. In the file
    pane's header they cannot be: the header is two lines by contract, and a
    record stating `related` and `depends-on` would add a row per field inside it.
    So the pane draws the status in the header and these below it, in a strip of
    their own, the way it already treats a file's frontmatter.

    Which is the whole of the split. Nothing here knows where it is being drawn.
    The list is the record's own order, and the questions about emptiness are the
    ones MetadataView was already asking — moved rather than rewritten, so a record
    renders the same rows in either host.

    ---

    The list below is indented as though it were still inside MetadataView's
    `@if`, and it has to stay that way. A newline between two sibling expressions
    is a text node in the rendered output, indentation included — Razor only trims
    the whitespace at an element's edges and around a code block — so the source
    indentation here is the whitespace between every row of every record this
    product ships. MetadataViewMarkupTests asserts those bytes exactly, which is
    how this was caught when the list first moved out and was re-indented to its
    new home. There is no visual difference either way; there is a diff, and a
    diff in shipped markup should mean somebody changed the markup.
*@

            <dl class="knowledge-fields" data-testid="@TestId">
                @RenderReferences("related", Metadata.Related)
                @RenderReferences("depends-on", Metadata.DependsOn)
                @RenderReferences("implements", Metadata.Implements)
                @RenderAliases()
                @RenderStrings("alternatives", Metadata.Alternatives)
                @RenderKind()
                @RenderVersion()
                @RenderIssue()
                @RenderEffort()
                @RenderKeys("roadmap", Metadata.Roadmap, "knowledge-roadmap-tags")
                @RenderKeys("feature-flag", Metadata.FeatureFlag, "knowledge-feature-flag-tags")

                @* Fields the schema does not define are shown as themselves. A
                   viewer that dropped them would make a genuine schema addition
                   look like a file that says nothing.

                   Inside a code block on purpose. Razor emits the whitespace
                   between two sibling expressions as content, and trims it either
                   side of a block, so the rows above are separated by their source
                   indentation and these are not — which is the markup this record
                   has always rendered. *@
                @if (Metadata.Extra.Count > 0)
                {
                    <MetadataExtraFields Extra="@Metadata.Extra" />
                }
            </dl>

@code {
    // Each field asks its own question about emptiness and then hands its value
    // to the component for that value's shape: an absent list, a blank scalar and
    // an estimate of zero are three different answers, and only the field knows
    // which it is looking at. What a row *is* — the pair, the label, the value
    // cell — is MetadataField's, and what a value *looks like* belongs to the
    // one component per shape beside this one. What is left here is the only thing
    // that is genuinely about the record: which fields are present, and in what
    // order the rows appear.
    //
    // The value component is written hard against the row's opening tag. Razor
    // emits the whitespace between an element's tag and a child element as
    // content, so a newline there would add it to every value cell in the product.
    private RenderFragment RenderReferences(string label, IReadOnlyList<KnowledgeReference> references) =>
        @<text>
            @if (references.Count > 0)
            {
                <MetadataField Label="@label"><MetadataReferenceList References="@references" HrefFor="@HrefFor" OnNavigate="@OnNavigate" /></MetadataField>
            }
        </text>;

    // Alternatives are plain strings by design — a technology that was weighed and
    // rejected has no chapter to point at — so they never become links, and they
    // must not be dressed as one either. MetadataValueList is the component that
    // cannot: it takes no href and no handler.
    //
    // Aliases used to be drawn through here too, and are not any more: the term an
    // alias names *is* modelled somewhere, so unlike an alternative it is a word a
    // host can be asked to place. See RenderAliases.
    private RenderFragment RenderStrings(string label, IReadOnlyList<string> values) =>
        @<text>
            @if (values.Count > 0)
            {
                <MetadataField Label="@label"><MetadataValueList Values="@values" /></MetadataField>
            }
        </text>;

    // An alias applies to a name rather than standing on its own, so it is drawn as
    // a qualifier: a badge set above the baseline and a step smaller than the text
    // it belongs to.
    //
    // The record wires no resolver. HrefFor answers for a KnowledgeReference and an
    // alias is not one — an alias is a word, which is the whole reason the field
    // holds plain strings — so there is nothing here to hand down. What a host would
    // resolve an alias *to* is a question no file in this repository answers today,
    // and a parameter for it would be this component claiming to know. The
    // capability lives on MetadataAliasList, where it can be looked at and tested,
    // until a naming index exists to wire it to.
    private RenderFragment RenderAliases() =>
        @<text>
            @if (Metadata.Aliases.Count > 0)
            {
                <MetadataField Label="aliases"><MetadataAliasList Aliases="@Metadata.Aliases" /></MetadataField>
            }
        </text>;

    // A closed vocabulary drawn as the classification it is, and the first of the
    // three rows that state their own field. The label is hidden rather than
    // dropped: a chip reading `framework` says what it is to somebody looking at it,
    // and says nothing at all to somebody listening.
    private RenderFragment RenderKind() =>
        @<text>
            @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Metadata.Kind))
            {
                <MetadataField Label="kind" ShowLabel="false"><MetadataKindBadge Kind="@Metadata.Kind" /></MetadataField>
            }
        </text>;

    // The same authored-scalar shape it has always been, with the `v` that makes it
    // legible without a label. Still a code element: `10.0` is a version string and
    // not the number ten, and the monospace face is what stops a reader rounding it.
    private RenderFragment RenderVersion() =>
        @<text>
            @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Metadata.Version))
            {
                <MetadataField Label="version" ShowLabel="false"><MetadataScalar Value="@Metadata.Version" Prefix="v" Title="@($"version: {Metadata.Version}")" /></MetadataField>
            }
        </text>;

    // An issue is a URL or the `owner/repo#number` shorthand, and which of the two
    // it is decides whether it can be followed. That question belongs to the value
    // component, so the record only asks whether the field was stated at all.
    private RenderFragment RenderIssue() =>
        @<text>
            @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Metadata.Issue))
            {
                <MetadataField Label="issue"><MetadataIssueLink Issue="@Metadata.Issue" /></MetadataField>
            }
        </text>;

    // Absent when there is no estimate; 0 is an estimate and still shows. The
    // record asks that question here rather than leaving the badge to answer it,
    // because it is the row that must not appear — a badge that drew nothing would
    // leave a labelled empty cell.
    //
    // The third labelless row. "5 pts" names its own field; "5" would not, which is
    // why the unit was already spelled out before the label went.
    private RenderFragment RenderEffort() =>
        @<text>
            @if (Metadata.Effort is not null)
            {
                <MetadataField Label="effort" ShowLabel="false"><MetadataEffortBadge Effort="@Metadata.Effort" /></MetadataField>
            }
        </text>;

    // Roadmap keys and feature-flag keys are the same shape: identifiers something
    // outside these files answers to, drawn in the feature-badge family and never
    // links. One fragment for both, so the two fields cannot drift apart — and the
    // test id lands on the value cell, which is what MetadataField's TestId is
    // for.
    private RenderFragment RenderKeys(string label, IReadOnlyList<string> keys, string testId) =>
        @<text>
            @if (keys.Count > 0)
            {
                <MetadataField Label="@label" TestId="@testId"><MetadataKeyList Keys="@keys" /></MetadataField>
            }
        </text>;

    /// <summary>The record whose fields these are.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public MetadataRecord Metadata { get; set; } = MetadataRecord.Empty;

    /// <summary>Raised with the reference a reader activated.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<KnowledgeReference> OnNavigate { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Resolves a reference to a link target; null leaves that reference
    /// as text rather than a broken link.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public Func<KnowledgeReference, string?>? HrefFor { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The list's own test id. Null leaves the attribute off, which is
    /// what <see cref="MetadataView"/> wants — the record it is inside is already
    /// identified.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? TestId { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Whether a record states anything but its status, and so whether
    /// this would draw any row at all.
    ///
    /// <para>Static, and asked by the host rather than answered by a component that
    /// renders nothing: an empty <c>&lt;dl&gt;</c> is a gap with a gap's spacing
    /// around it, and the hosts need the answer before they commit to the element
    /// that would wrap it — a record's own group in
    /// <see cref="MetadataView"/>, a strip of its own in the file pane.</para></summary>
    public static bool HasAny(MetadataRecord metadata) =>
        metadata.Related.Count > 0
        || metadata.DependsOn.Count > 0
        || metadata.Implements.Count > 0
        || metadata.Aliases.Count > 0
        || metadata.Alternatives.Count > 0
        || !string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(metadata.Kind)
        || !string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(metadata.Version)
        || !string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(metadata.Issue)
        || metadata.Effort is not null
        || metadata.Roadmap.Count > 0
        || metadata.FeatureFlag.Count > 0
        || metadata.Extra.Count > 0;
}
